{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age when his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem ruled as regents until he came of age. Raymond III was involved in the politics of the Crusader states from an early age.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a key figure in the succession crisis after the death of King Baldwin IV in 1185. Raymond III was appointed as regent for Baldwin V who was the designated heir to the throne. However Baldwin V died in 1186 and Raymond III initially opposed the coronation of Guy of Lusignan as king. Raymond III supported Sibylla and her husband Guy but later changed his allegiance when Guy was captured at the Battle of Hattin in 1187. After the battle Raymond III was one of the few nobles to escape and he became a leading figure in the resistance against Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III was also involved in the politics of the County of Tripoli. He worked to strengthen the county and maintain its independence from the Principality of Antioch. Raymond III also had a complex relationship with the Knights Templar. He was initially a supporter of the order but later clashed with them over control of the city of Tortosa. Raymond III also had a dispute with Bohemond III of Antioch over the succession of the County of Tripoli. Raymond III was captured by Bohemond III in 1165 and was held prisoner until 1173. Despite this Raymond III continued to play a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. His 